31
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.659
So what is this real-time physically-based rendering

32
00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:35.060
we've been mentioning since the beginning of class?

33
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.939
Physically-based rendering relies on a unified light

34
00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:40.340
and shading system.

35
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.049
All right, but what does this mean?

36
00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:45.810
It means that surfaces in real-time PBR

37
00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.129
approximate how light behaves in the real world.

38
00:01:49.129 --> 00:01:51.329
Physically-based materials tend to be more accurate

39
00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.890
because they can be measured in lumens,

40
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.769
lux, inverse square law, energy conservation, et cetera,

41
00:01:57.769 --> 00:01:58.969
et cetera.

42
00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:02.129
And this approach tends to be more natural-looking

43
00:02:02.129 --> 00:02:06.430
and is actually more intuitive and consistent.

44
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.110
So putting it down visually, the approximation

45
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.509
and unification of light and shade of TBRs

46
00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:15.430
can be categorized into non-metallic and metallic

47
00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.430
materials, as we can see in this slide.

48
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.530
Non-metallic surfaces are characterized

49
00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:25.250
by their matte colors and absence of light reflectivity,

50
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.889
whereas metallic surfaces are highly reflective, as we know.

51
00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:32.330
In these two charts on the left and right side

52
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.689
of the slide, you can see the RGB values

53
00:02:34.689 --> 00:02:36.189
of real-life materials.

54
00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.530
But take into consideration that the RGB

55
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.889
is actually a digital representation of color

56
00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:44.330
using a finite set of numbers, right?

57
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.530
RGB is an additive color model,

58
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:50.530
which means we start with black, the absence of light,

59
00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.310
and then add different amounts of red, green, and blue

60
00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.469
to create a full spectrum of colors.

61
00:02:56.469 --> 00:02:58.169
And here on the left, you can actually see

62
00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:00.849
how different real-life materials like charcoal,

63
00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:04.449
dry, dark earth, or asphalt are translated

64
00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:08.050
to the digital world using this RGB system.

65
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.409
Keep in mind that in real life,

66
00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:12.129
there are no full blacks nor whites,

67
00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:16.770
and the color information of a surface always has a range.

68
00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.210
And roughness is a fundamental factor

69
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.530
in the distinction between different materials.

70
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.069
It's going to define whether a surface is, for example,

71
00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:28.719
made of metal, leather, or cloth.

72
00:03:28.719 --> 00:03:30.300
And speaking about roughness,

73
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.460
remember that the specular input in a material

74
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.360
will define also its reflectivity.

75
00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.960
So it's important to find the right balance

76
00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.560
between the two, between roughness and specular,

77
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.240
even though for most non-metallic materials,

78
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.479
the default value of 0.5 specular

79
00:03:44.479 --> 00:03:46.740
is usually physically correct.

80
00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.400
Okay, so here you can find an overview

81
00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.229
of the metallic range from 0 to 1.

82
00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:55.389
And here, you can see a chart expanding

83
00:03:55.389 --> 00:03:58.509
upon the 0 to 1 roughness range of a material,

84
00:03:58.509 --> 00:04:02.189
defining how the light gets reflected on its surface.

85
00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:03.430
And it can be a bit confusing

86
00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.110
because it looks like the opposite of the metallic range

87
00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.509
because a metallic value of 0

88
00:04:09.509 --> 00:04:12.129
means that a material is not metallic at all,

89
00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:14.210
while a metallic value of 1,

90
00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.269
as on the right side of this chart,

91
00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:19.860
means that a material is fully metallic,

92
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.100
while the roughness for it is opposite,

93
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.180
because a material with a roughness value of 1

94
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.579
has the light scattered in multiple directions,

95
00:04:28.579 --> 00:04:31.019
making the material matte,

96
00:04:31.019 --> 00:04:34.500
while smooth materials with a roughness value of 0

97
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.779
have the light bounced off at the same angle

98
00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:38.980
in a single direction towards us,

99
00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:42.160
creating a mirror-like surface.

100
